    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d184">

                <pb facs="0330" n="298" xml:id="pg298"/>

                <head>184. Telegram From the Embassy in the United Kingdom to the Department of
                        State<anchor n="1" xml:id="d184-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1958-12-17T17:00:00+00:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 17,
                                1958—5 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>3237. During question period Commons December 15, opposition claiming Cuban
                    rebels being killed by arms supplied by UK, badgered Minister State Foreign
                    Affairs (Noble) into giving assurance “no further weapons of any sort will be
                    sent (to Cuban Government) without informing Commons.”</p>
                <p>Assistant head American Department Foreign Office (Hildyard) informed Embassy
                    officer December 16 it not yet decided whether this assurance will include arms
                    already loaded on ships, or contracted for, or only to new orders.</p>
                <p>Foreign Office said assurance given primarily as concession UK public opinion and
                    not indication HMG foresees rebel victory. This possibility not overlooked by
                    Foreign Office, however, particularly if Cuban economic situation
                    deteriorates.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Whitney</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
